            "time": "2026-11-21T06:01:04Z",
            "attachment_id": {},
            "bug_id": 19356,
            "text": "## Summary\n\nAfter any read of its data, such as `sum(x)` or `identical(x, y)`, a `sort()` result loses its sortedness and no-NA metadata, so `is.unsorted(x)` and `anyNA(x)` scan the whole vector again. Fixes a reproducible slowdown relative to R 4.6.1; results do not change.\n\n## Details\n\nSince r90261, the read-only `Dataptr` and `Dataptr_or_null` wrapper methods (`src/main/altclasses.c`) go through `WRAPPER_WRAPPED_RW()`, which also resets the metadata; only a write needs that. Seen at r90952; the R 4.6.2 sources have the same code.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `wrapper-readonly-metadata-repro.R`; after `sum(x)` on 5e7 sorted doubles, 20 calls each of `is.unsorted(x)` and `anyNA(x)` take about 0.5 s and 0.25 s, against under 0.01 s in R 4.6.1 and with the patch.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `wrapper-readonly-metadata.diff`; the read-only methods now only duplicate and lock the data, and writable requests still reset the metadata. The copy on the first read of shared data (r90309) is unchanged. A regression test is included, and `make check` passes on macOS arm64.\n\nIf this is ported to R-patched, it needs r90948 (PR#19354) there as well: without it, a wrapper that is read and then serialized fails on its next access. Trunk already has r90948.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/521, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while investigating PR#19287.",
